When to use @future

Four situations, in rough order of how often they actually come up:

Callouts from triggers. A trigger runs inside an open transaction with uncommitted DML. Salesforce blocks HTTP callouts in that state — You have uncommitted work pending. Deferring the callout to a future method runs it in a fresh transaction after commit.

Isolating mixed DML (detail below).

Escaping synchronous governor limits. Async context gets its own bucket with higher ceilings: 12 MB heap vs 6, 60s CPU vs 10, 200 callouts vs 100. If a synchronous path is brushing against CPU time, moving the heavy part async buys headroom.

Fire-and-forget work where the user shouldn't wait — audit logging, notification dispatch, cache priming, syncing to an external system.

Caveat worth repeating: for anything new, Queueable covers all of these and does it better (non-primitive params, job ID for monitoring, chaining). @future survives mainly because it's a one-liner from a trigger with no class instantiation.

