Mixed DML error

Salesforce splits objects into two categories:

Setup objects — User, UserRole, Group, GroupMember, Permission Set, PermissionSetAssignment, QueueSObject, Territory, FieldPermissions, ObjectPermissions
Non-setup objects — Account, Contact, Opportunity, all custom objects

You cannot perform DML on both categories in the same transaction:

MIXED_DML_OPERATION: DML operation on setup object is not permitted 
after you have updated a non-setup object (or vice versa)

The underlying reason is that setup and non-setup data live in different storage with different commit semantics — Salesforce can't guarantee an atomic rollback across both, so it refuses the combination outright rather than risk a partial commit.

How @future fixes it: the future method executes in its own separate transaction. The two DML operations are never co-resident, so the restriction never applies.

apex
public class OnboardingService {

    public static void onboard(Contact c, Id userId) {
        insert c;                          // non-setup DML
        assignPermSet(userId);             // deferred → separate transaction
    }

    @future
    private static void assignPermSet(Id userId) {
        PermissionSet ps = [SELECT Id FROM PermissionSet WHERE Name = 'Portal_Access'];
        insert new PermissionSetAssignment(
            AssigneeId = userId,
            PermissionSetId = ps.Id
        );                                 // setup DML, now isolated
    }
}

Two things to flag:

Queueable solves this identically, and gives you a job ID so you can tell whether the second half actually succeeded. With @future, if the permission set assignment fails, the caller has no idea.
In tests only, System.runAs() also sidesteps mixed DML by resetting the transaction context. It's a test-only tool — it won't help in production code.

